WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.302
We counted that maxSubArraySum costs at most

00:00:04.387 --> 00:00:07.708
3n² + 8n + 5

00:00:07.708 --> 00:00:11.455
and we said that this grows like n².

00:00:11.455 --> 00:00:14.180
Now we make "grows like" precise.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:18.216
Asymptotic describes how a function behaves

00:00:18.301 --> 00:00:21.816
as its variable approaches a limit: here

00:00:21.816 --> 00:00:23.787
as n goes to infinity.

00:00:23.873 --> 00:00:26.530
We compare running times by how

00:00:26.616 --> 00:00:28.930
fast they grow for large n.

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:31.718
Here is the definition.

00:00:31.814 --> 00:00:35.174
g(n) is O(f(n)) if there

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.398
exist two positive constants, c and n₀

00:00:39.398 --> 00:00:43.622
such that g(n) is at most c * f(n)

00:00:43.622 --> 00:00:47.750
for all n ≥ n₀.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.230
Read it as

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.440
g grows asymptotically no faster than f.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.136
Usually g is the complicated count

00:00:57.136 --> 00:00:59.704
and f a simple function

00:00:59.704 --> 00:01:01.860
like n or n².

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.061
Each part throws something away.

00:01:05.061 --> 00:01:07.377
For all n from n₀ on

00:01:07.377 --> 00:01:10.316
we only care about large inputs.

00:01:10.316 --> 00:01:13.523
Below n₀, g may even be bigger.

00:01:13.523 --> 00:01:15.572
And "at most c * f"

00:01:15.572 --> 00:01:17.710
g may be bigger than f

00:01:17.799 --> 00:01:20.471
as long as it stays below some

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.698
constant multiple of it.

00:01:22.698 --> 00:01:24.302
Constant factors

00:01:24.302 --> 00:01:27.420
like the 3 in 3n²

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.250
or the speed of your machine, are ignored.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.602
To prove an O claim

00:01:33.602 --> 00:01:36.189
you must exhibit c and n₀

00:01:36.272 --> 00:01:38.525
and show the inequality for

00:01:38.608 --> 00:01:40.527
every n from n₀ on.

00:01:40.527 --> 00:01:44.281
To disprove it, you must show that no choice

00:01:44.365 --> 00:01:46.200
of c and n₀ works.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:47.774
Our example:

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:50.591
3n² + 8n +

00:01:50.674 --> 00:01:52.994
5 is O(n²).

00:01:52.994 --> 00:01:53.906
Here is g.

00:01:53.906 --> 00:01:56.474
And here is 16n²

00:01:56.557 --> 00:01:59.126
which stays above it everywhere

00:01:59.209 --> 00:02:00.700
from n = 1.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.148
Why 16?

00:02:02.148 --> 00:02:03.978
For n ≥ 1,

00:02:03.978 --> 00:02:07.271
8n is at most 8n²

00:02:07.271 --> 00:02:10.565
and 5 is at most 5n².

00:02:10.656 --> 00:02:14.407
So g(n) is at most 3n² +

00:02:14.498 --> 00:02:17.792
8n² + 5n²

00:02:17.792 --> 00:02:19.530
16n².

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.043
So c = 16 and n

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:23.947
₀ = 1 work.

00:02:23.947 --> 00:02:26.456
The vertical axis is scaled,

00:02:26.456 --> 00:02:28.360
the shapes are exact.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.114
Other pairs work too,

00:02:31.114 --> 00:02:35.152
but a smaller c needs a larger n₀.

00:02:35.152 --> 00:02:37.222
With c = 4,

00:02:37.326 --> 00:02:41.985
from which n on is 3n² + 8

00:02:42.088 --> 00:02:45.712
n + 5 at most 4n²?

00:02:45.712 --> 00:02:48.300
And with c = 3?

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.770
Pause the video and find out.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.135
Take c = 4.

00:02:56.135 --> 00:02:59.047
4n² starts below g

00:02:59.047 --> 00:03:02.710
and crosses it between 8 and 9.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:03.620
Indeed,

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.980
3n² + 8n + 5 at

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.340
most 4n² means n² -

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:13.300
8n - 5 is at least 0,

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.100
which holds from n = 9 on.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.020
So c = 4 works

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.020
with n₀ = 9.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.384
Now lower c to 3.

00:03:22.384 --> 00:03:25.742
The curve cn² slides down

00:03:25.742 --> 00:03:28.332
and never catches g again.

00:03:28.332 --> 00:03:31.881
With c = 3, no n₀ works

00:03:31.977 --> 00:03:35.238
because 3n² + 8

00:03:35.334 --> 00:03:38.404
n + 5 is bigger than 3

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.610
n² for every n.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:44.768
A program can search for n₀.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.675
FindN0 defines g(n) and

00:03:47.787 --> 00:03:50.470
f(n) = n².

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:51.793
For each c,

00:03:51.793 --> 00:03:55.417
it checks every n from 1 to 1,000,000

00:03:55.506 --> 00:03:58.865
and records the last n where g(n) is

00:03:58.954 --> 00:04:01.871
still bigger than c * f(n).

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.258
One after the last failure

00:04:04.347 --> 00:04:06.380
is the smallest n₀.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.873
The output agrees with the algebra.

00:04:09.956 --> 00:04:12.369
c = 3 fails at every

00:04:12.453 --> 00:04:13.950
n up to 1,000,000.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:16.780
c = 4 works from 9 on.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:19.609
5 from 5, 8 from 3,

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:21.440
and 16 from 1.

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:25.035
A check up to 1,000,000 is evidence,

00:04:25.035 --> 00:04:26.207
not a proof.

00:04:26.207 --> 00:04:28.550
The algebra is the proof.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.628
And notice what the program records:

00:04:31.628 --> 00:04:34.734
the last failure, not the first success.

00:04:34.734 --> 00:04:37.462
Two curves can cross several times.

00:04:37.538 --> 00:04:40.948
n₀ is the point from which the inequality

00:04:41.023 --> 00:04:42.918
holds for every larger n

00:04:42.918 --> 00:04:46.100
not the first n where it happens to hold.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.300
Here is another way to see

00:04:49.401 --> 00:04:51.920
all five answers at once.

00:04:52.021 --> 00:04:55.850
g(n) at most c n² is the same

00:04:55.951 --> 00:04:59.478
as g(n) over n² at most c.

00:04:59.478 --> 00:05:01.392
So draw the ratio

00:05:01.392 --> 00:05:04.113
which falls towards 3

00:05:04.113 --> 00:05:07.640
and a horizontal line at height c.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.808
With c equals 16

00:05:09.891 --> 00:05:12.121
the curve is under the line

00:05:12.204 --> 00:05:13.691
from n equals 1.

00:05:13.691 --> 00:05:15.757
Lower the line to 8

00:05:15.757 --> 00:05:19.310
and the curve gets under it from 3 on.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:20.300
At 5

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.340
the curve gets under it from 5 on.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.780
At 4, from 9 on: the lower the line

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:29.580
the later the curve gets under it.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:33.202
And at 3, the line is exactly the level

00:05:33.278 --> 00:05:36.321
the ratio approaches, but never reaches.

00:05:36.321 --> 00:05:38.680
The curve never gets under it

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.810
so no starting point works.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.250
Two more examples.

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:46.454
A linear function, a n + b

00:05:46.454 --> 00:05:50.898
with a and b not negative, is O(n).

00:05:50.898 --> 00:05:54.825
For n at least 1, b is at most b n

00:05:54.825 --> 00:05:59.372
so a n + b is at most (a + b) * n.

00:05:59.372 --> 00:06:01.852
Take c equals a + b

00:06:01.852 --> 00:06:04.229
and n₀ equals 1.

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:06.916
If a and b are both 0

00:06:06.916 --> 00:06:09.706
take c equals 1 instead

00:06:09.706 --> 00:06:12.910
the constant must be positive.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.295
So the sum of an array, 4 n + 4

00:06:17.295 --> 00:06:18.869
is O(n)

00:06:18.869 --> 00:06:23.100
with c equals 8 and n₀ equals 1.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.961
Second: logarithms in different bases.

00:06:26.961 --> 00:06:29.378
Here are log₂ n

00:06:29.378 --> 00:06:31.364
and log₁₀ n

00:06:31.364 --> 00:06:33.436
for n up to 1000.

00:06:33.436 --> 00:06:35.680
They have the same shape.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:39.222
By the change of base formula

00:06:39.222 --> 00:06:43.053
logₐ n is logb n

00:06:43.053 --> 00:06:46.032
divided by logb a

00:06:46.032 --> 00:06:50.713
and logb a is a positive constant.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:53.799
So logₐ n is O

00:06:53.905 --> 00:06:55.927
of logb n

00:06:55.927 --> 00:07:00.183
take c equals 1 over logb a

00:07:00.183 --> 00:07:02.630
and n₀ equals 1.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:06.285
The program LogBase prints both logs

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:08.761
for n from 10 to 1 billion

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:11.835
and their ratio is always the same

00:07:11.835 --> 00:07:14.568
3.3219

00:07:14.568 --> 00:07:17.130
which is log₂ 10.

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:19.983
So the base of a logarithm does

00:07:20.063 --> 00:07:22.081
not matter inside Big-Oh.

00:07:22.162 --> 00:07:24.584
Log₂ n and log

00:07:24.665 --> 00:07:26.925
₁₀ n are the same class

00:07:26.925 --> 00:07:29.508
and we simply write O(log n).

00:07:29.508 --> 00:07:30.800
In this course,

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:33.302
log n means log₂ n

00:07:33.302 --> 00:07:35.320
unless we say otherwise.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:40.495
Practice: is 2n Big-Oh of n²?

00:07:40.495 --> 00:07:45.443
We need 2n ≤ cn², which,

00:07:45.443 --> 00:07:49.567
dividing by n, is 2 ≤ cn.

00:07:49.567 --> 00:07:54.398
Take c = 2 and n₀ = 1.

00:07:54.398 --> 00:07:55.340
So yes.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:58.416
But isn't 2n Big-Oh of n?

00:07:58.416 --> 00:08:01.434
It is, also with c = 2.

00:08:01.434 --> 00:08:05.328
Both are true, and so are O(n log n),

00:08:05.328 --> 00:08:08.930
O(n³), and O(2ⁿ).

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.639
Big-Oh is only an upper bound

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:13.265
on the growth rate

00:08:13.265 --> 00:08:16.355
and a function has many upper bounds.

00:08:16.437 --> 00:08:18.470
Saying linearSearch is O

00:08:18.551 --> 00:08:21.560
(2ⁿ) is true, but useless.

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.282
We want the tightest upper bound

00:08:24.282 --> 00:08:26.798
and to know that a bound is tight

00:08:26.798 --> 00:08:28.811
we also need a lower bound.

00:08:28.811 --> 00:08:31.830
That is Big-Omega, later in this lecture.

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:34.942
A warning about the notation.

00:08:34.942 --> 00:08:37.126
2n is O(n²)

00:08:37.126 --> 00:08:40.531
and 3n² is O(n²).

00:08:40.531 --> 00:08:44.460
Does that mean 2n = 3n²?

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:45.770
Of course not.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.793
The equals sign in g(n) =

00:08:48.876 --> 00:08:51.298
O(f(n)) is just notation.

00:08:51.298 --> 00:08:52.551
Read it as:

00:08:52.634 --> 00:08:55.391
g belongs to the set of functions

00:08:55.474 --> 00:08:57.729
that grow no faster than f.

00:08:57.729 --> 00:09:01.320
Some books write it with the element sign.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:04.258
So such a statement only reads

00:09:04.345 --> 00:09:05.984
from left to right.

00:09:05.984 --> 00:09:09.176
2n = O(n²) is true,

00:09:09.262 --> 00:09:11.419
but O(n²) =

00:09:11.505 --> 00:09:13.230
2n means nothing.

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:14.801
To sum up.

00:09:14.801 --> 00:09:15.301
1.

00:09:15.395 --> 00:09:20.052
g(n) is Big-Oh of f(n) if g(n) ≤

00:09:20.151 --> 00:09:24.114
c * f(n) for all n ≥ n₀

00:09:24.114 --> 00:09:27.285
for some positive c and n₀.

00:09:27.285 --> 00:09:31.446
2: prove it by exhibiting c and n₀.

00:09:31.446 --> 00:09:34.914
A smaller c needs a larger n₀

00:09:34.914 --> 00:09:38.778
and a c that is too small never works.

00:09:38.778 --> 00:09:42.741
3: Constant factors and the base of

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.416
a logarithm do not matter.

00:09:45.416 --> 00:09:49.874
O is an upper bound, and we read it as

00:09:49.874 --> 00:09:52.450
g is in the class O of f.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:53.633
Try this:

00:09:53.725 --> 00:09:57.055
prove that 5n² + 3

00:09:57.148 --> 00:09:59.460
n is O(n²)

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:01.680
by giving c and n₀.

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:05.317
Next: 5 rules that simplify a running

00:10:05.399 --> 00:10:07.043
time in a few steps

00:10:07.043 --> 00:10:09.590
and the common growth classes.

